<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jinasena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jinasena was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> Jain monk and scholar of the ninth century, revered as teacher of the Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a>. He completed the Jayadhavala commentary on Jain scripture begun by his own teacher Virasena and began the Adipurana, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> life of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>, Rishabhanatha, which his disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunabhadra">Gunabhadra</a> finished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jinasena.jpg" alt="Jinasena" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: जैन · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Digambar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jinasena belonged to the Panchastupanvaya, a lineage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> monks that tradition traces from Chandrasena, who initiated Aryanandi, who in turn initiated Virasena and Jayasena. Jinasena was one of Virasena's six disciples, trained by him in Jain doctrine and the interpretation of scripture, and after his teacher's death he carried on and widened his scholarly work in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. With his fellow disciple Dasharayguru he initiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunabhadra">Gunabhadra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Adipurana, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> mahākāvya, tells of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>, Rishabhanatha, and his sons Bharata and Bahubali, and stresses ahimsa, renunciation and spiritual liberation. It forms part of the Mahapurana, on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunabhadra">Gunabhadra</a> also worked, and it served as the model for the vernacular Adipurana of the 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a>. Jinasena tells the Jain tradition that Rishabhanatha first taught humanity to extract <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> juice. A long passage of the Mahapurana rejects the idea that a creator made the world, arguing that the world is uncreated and without beginning or end; Carl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Sagan</a> was among those who later quoted it. He also wrote a lawbook for laymen, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra">Dharmashastra</a>.</p><p>Jinasena barred meat, honey and similar materials from Jain rituals because of their link with violence, preached the importance of dāna, charity, for Jain households, and forbade the sacred thread to artisans, dancers and shudras. He described a twice-born class founded on conduct rather than birth. The scholar Padmanabh Jaini traces to such teaching the Jaina brahmans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambaras</a>, who later hardened into a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As rajaguru, royal preceptor, of the Rashtrakuta emperor Amoghavarsha, Jinasena shaped the religious life of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Tradition holds that the king began each day at court by saluting in the direction of his guru's monastery, and that after a reign of 64 years he gave the throne to his son Krishna II and spent his last years in retreat under Jinasena's guidance. His works became central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> teaching.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jinasena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Amoghavarsha I the Jain teachers <b>Jinasena</b> and Gunabhadra composed the Adipurana, and the mathematician Mahaviracharya wrote the Ganitasarasangraha c. 850.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
